---
/* THE TERMS, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT A SET OF GENERAL TERMS AND CONDITIONS.

   The footer named this route Terms, and the obvious thing to put behind it
   was the AGB skeleton the launch gate ships. It does not fit, for a reason
   that is worth writing down rather than rediscovering.

   AGB govern a contract. Nothing on this site concludes one: there is no
   purchase, no account, no sign-up, no download and, today, no release. Terms
   drafted for a contract that does not exist would have to invent an ordering
   process, a payment term and a termination notice for a page whose only two
   actions are joining a chat room and reading source. They would also come
   with a real cost: general terms are subject to content review under sections
   305 to 310 BGB, and a clause that fails does not soften, it disappears and
   the statute takes its place. Terms nobody needed can leave the writer worse
   off than none at all.

   What this route is instead: the conditions under which this site is offered,
   plus the pointer to the licence that actually governs the software. Both are
   true today and neither pretends to a relationship the reader is not in.

   THE MOMENT THIS PAGE BECOMES INSUFFICIENT is the moment something here is
   sold, subscribed to, or accounts for. On that day this is replaced by real
   terms with legal review behind them, and not extended clause by clause.
   ADR 0258 carries the derivation.

   NO LINK DISCLAIMER AND NO LIABILITY BOILERPLATE, for the reason the imprint
   gives: those blocks change nothing and read as copied.

   THE FONT LICENCES ARE NAMED ONE BY ONE AND NOT COLLAPSED INTO A FAMILY. An
   earlier draft of this page said the typefaces here are licensed under the SIL
   Open Font License 1.1, which is true of Archivo and IBM Plex Mono and false
   of Zodiak: that one comes from the Indian Type Foundry through Fontshare,
   under the foundry's own licence. Two of three sharing a licence is how the
   collapse happened, and a licence statement that is wrong is worse than no
   statement at all, because it is the sentence somebody relies on. The notice
   served beside the font files is the list, and this page points at it rather
   than restating it. */
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { ENTITY } from '../lib/legal';
import { LICENCE_ID, LICENCE_URL, ORG, REPO, SITE } from '../lib/site';
---
<Legal
  title="Terms"
  lede="The conditions this site is offered under, and the licence that governs the software it describes."
>
  <h2>What these terms cover</h2>
  <p>
    They cover this website. They are not a contract: nothing here is sold,
    nothing is subscribed to, and there is no account to open. The software
    itself is governed by its licence rather than by this page, and the two are
    kept apart on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>The software</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is free software licensed under{' '}
    <a href={LICENCE_URL}>{LICENCE_ID}</a>. That licence, and not this page,
    states what you may do with the program: use it, study it, change it and
    pass it on, under the conditions it sets out. Its own sections 15 and 16
    disclaim warranty and limit liability, and they apply as written to the
    extent the law allows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The source is at <a href={REPO}>{REPO.replace('https://', '')}</a>. If the
    licence and anything on this site disagree, the licence is what governs.
  </p>

  <h2>This site</h2>
  <p>
    The site's own source is part of the same repository and carries the same
    licence. Its text describes software under development. There is no release
    yet, no date for one, and nothing on this site is an offer, a promise of a
    feature, or a commitment to a schedule.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not promise that the site is reachable or complete at any given
    moment, and we may change or remove any part of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Names and logos</h2>
  <p>
    The licence covers the software, not the naming. {SITE.name},{' '}
    {ORG.name} and {ORG.parent.name}, together with their logos and wordmarks,
    are not licensed by it. You may of course name the project, refer to it and
    link to it; what needs our agreement is using the marks in a way that
    suggests a fork, a rebuild or a separate product is ours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third-party marks shown on this site belong to their respective owners and
    are used to identify the platforms and systems they stand for.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party material</h2>
  <p>
    The typefaces served here are not all under the same licence. Archivo and
    IBM Plex Mono are licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1; Zodiak comes
    from the Indian Type Foundry through Fontshare and is licensed under that
    foundry's own terms. The{' '}
    <a href="/fonts/NOTICE.txt">notice served with the font files</a> names each
    face and the licence it is under. The icon sets the site draws from are
    credited with their licences in the source, at the module that reads each
    one.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    We may change this page when what it describes changes. The date at the
    foot of it says when the whole of it was last checked.
  </p>

  <h2>Applicable law</h2>
  <p>
    German law applies. Where you are a consumer resident in another country,
    the mandatory protections of that country's law remain unaffected.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    {ENTITY.name}, {ENTITY.holder}. The full details are in the{' '}
    <a href="/imprint/">imprint</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
